1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,580
TermClaw is an open-source desktop app

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,970
for managing multiple
remote terminal sessions.

3
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:07,380
Connect to different machines over SSH

4
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:10,290
and run AI coding agents
in parallel across projects.

5
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:13,450
Review their changes, manage remote
Docker containers,

6
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,650
or open the same workspace in a browser.

7
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:18,450
Let's follow a checkout worker
stuck in a retry loop.

8
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:22,910
In TermClaw, the developer, the
reviewer,

9
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:26,270
and the worker logs can each have their
own SSH session.

10
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:27,610
Tile them together.

11
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:31,030
With tmux, a dropped connection doesn't
interrupt the job.

12
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:33,890
Close the desktop window.

13
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:36,550
With background mode on, the sessions
keep running.

14
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:39,840
Open the token-protected web interface
and pick up the same work in a

15
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:40,250
browser.

16
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:42,150
You can even start TermClaw hidden.

17
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:46,290
From your phone, ask Claude to check the
worker.

18
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:50,710
Its connector talks to TermClaw's MCP
server, finds the shared session,

19
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,750
sends your request, and reads back the
agent's findings.

20
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,210
Claude Code, Codex, and other MCP
clients can use those same tools.

21
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:01,770
Need another agent on a remote machine?

22
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,290
Open the Agents menu and choose an
installer.

23
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:07,910
TermClaw runs the official install
command in that project's terminal.

24
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,310
Deploy the team's skills and MCP servers
there, too.

25
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:16,920
OpenCode and GitHub Copilot also connect
through the Agent Client

26
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:17,430
Protocol.

27
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:21,500
Open a chat, watch the tool calls
arrive, inspect a proposed diff,

28
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:23,890
and answer a permission request in
place.

29
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:28,130
Give an orchestrator a developer and a
reviewer to watch.

30
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:31,450
When the developer finishes, its output
goes to the coordinator.

31
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:33,900
The coordinator can ask the reviewer to
check the change,

32
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:35,470
with a round limit you control.

33
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:39,570
Open Files and browse the remote
machine.

34
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:42,770
Here, the checkout project has a reports
folder.

35
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,760
Open it, preview the retry report, and
download a copy to your computer.

36
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:49,710
Now upload the team's runbook into that
same folder.

37
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:53,220
Open the Markdown preview, edit the
handoff note, and save it back

38
00:01:53,220 --> 00:01:53,870
to the host.

39
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:56,390
Now open Git.

40
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,550
Choose the repository, inspect the
changed lines,

41
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:01,910
then select the files that belong in the
commit.

42
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:05,970
Branches, history, and pull request
checks are close at hand.

43
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:10,790
The checkout containers run on a
different machine, edge-03.

44
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:13,500
Open Docker in its TermClaw project.

45
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:18,320
TermClaw uses the project's SSH
connection to reach the Docker engine on

46
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:19,250
that Linux host.

47
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:23,450
Sort its containers by CPU and open the
worker's live logs.

48
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:27,890
From your laptop, select Restart on the
checkout worker.

49
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:30,090
Watch its status on the remote host.

50
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:32,610
The worker comes back up with a fresh
uptime.

51
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:34,850
The API and cache keep running.

52
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:40,390
On that same remote Docker host, open a
shell inside a container,

53
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:44,670
browse a volume read only, or manage
several containers at once.

54
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:48,550
You can also clean up unused images and
volumes over SSH.

55
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:53,170
Switch to Monitor to check the Linux
host beneath those containers.

56
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,390
Compare CPU and memory with disk and
network activity.

57
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:59,370
Sort the processes to see which one is
consuming the machine.

58
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:04,110
TermClaw brings your remote sessions,
browser access,

59
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,010
and mobile agent controls together.

60
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:10,910
Install agents, coordinate reviews, move
files, inspect Git changes,

61
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:14,270
manage remote Docker hosts, and monitor
your hosts.

62
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,070
It's open source under the MIT license.

63
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:18,970
Find TermClaw on GitHub.
